CREATE OR REPLACE PROCEDURE harness_app.archive_old_orders (
    p_order_date IN DATE
)
AS
    TYPE t_orders IS TABLE OF HARNESS_APP.ORDERS%ROWTYPE;
    l_orders t_orders;

    CURSOR c_old_orders IS
        SELECT o.*
          FROM HARNESS_APP.ORDERS o
         WHERE o.ORDER_DATE < p_order_date
         ORDER BY o.ORDER_ID;
BEGIN
    OPEN c_old_orders;

    BEGIN
        LOOP
            FETCH c_old_orders BULK COLLECT INTO l_orders LIMIT 10000;
            EXIT WHEN l_orders.COUNT = 0;

            FORALL i IN INDICES OF l_orders
                INSERT INTO HARNESS_APP.ORDERS_ARCHIVE
                VALUES l_orders(i);

            FORALL i IN INDICES OF l_orders
                DELETE FROM HARNESS_APP.ORDERS
                 WHERE ORDER_ID = l_orders(i).ORDER_ID;
        END LOOP;
    EXCEPTION
        WHEN OTHERS THEN
            IF c_old_orders%ISOPEN THEN
                CLOSE c_old_orders;
            END IF;
            RAISE;
    END;

    IF c_old_orders%ISOPEN THEN
        CLOSE c_old_orders;
    END IF;
END archive_old_orders;
/
```

Assumptions:

- “Older than a given date” is implemented as `ORDER_DATE < p_order_date`. If you mean “on or before”, the predicate would need to be `ORDER_DATE <= p_order_date`.
- The procedure does not issue `COMMIT` or `ROLLBACK`; the caller controls the transaction.
- `HARNESS_APP.ORDERS_ARCHIVE` has the same column order and compatible types as `HARNESS_APP.ORDERS`, so a record insert is valid.
- No row in `HARNESS_APP.ORDERS_ARCHIVE` already has the same `ORDER_ID` as a row being archived. If duplicates are possible, the archive step would need different handling.
- The cursor is ordered by `ORDER_ID`, which is unique, so batch boundaries are deterministic and there are no ties to break.

